  "answer": "Teriflunomide-driven DHODH blockade differentiates oxidative, cytarabine-residual AML with limited pyrimidine salvage\n\nHypothesis. In cytarabine-persistent AML with independently demonstrated high oxidative metabolism and limited uridine salvage, teriflunomide at human-relevant unbound exposure will inhibit DHODH-dependent pyrimidine production, induce durable myeloid differentiation, and reduce leukemia-propagating capacity more than normal hematopoietic progenitor function. This is one context-specific hypothesis, not a prediction for all AML or a claim that teriflunomide has never been investigated in leukemia.\n\nSupported premises. Cytarabine-residual AML cells in the studied models displayed oxidative-metabolism-associated features but were not consistently enriched for leukemia stem cells [S2]. Consequently, the proposed population should not be identified simply by the ROS-low phenotype associated with functionally defined leukemia stem cells in S1 [S1]. DHODH inhibition promoted differentiation and affected disease-related endpoints in experimental AML systems, supporting differentiation as a testable outcome distinct from nonspecific killing [S4]. Venetoclax–azacitidine provides an established metabolic comparator, not the proposed repurposing discovery [S3]. None of these sources establishes teriflunomide efficacy, exposure adequacy, or selectivity in this context.\n\nDrug identity and speculative mechanism. Unverified prior knowledge, requiring independent checking: teriflunomide is approved for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis, inhibits DHODH, and can restrict de novo pyrimidine synthesis. Approval wording, biochemical potency, protein binding, and clinically attainable unbound exposure are not verified by this packet. The speculative bridge is that a subset of oxidative residual AML remains dependent on de novo pyrimidines because salvage cannot compensate, and that restricting this supply permits differentiation rather than merely arresting proliferation. High oxygen consumption alone does not establish this dependency. Preferential preservation of normal progenitors is a further prediction to test, not an assumed consequence of pathway targeting.\n\nDiscriminating preclinical strategy. Obtain paired pretreatment and cytarabine-persistent primary AML samples, supplemented by matched patient-derived xenografts. Before teriflunomide exposure, classify residual cells using cell-normalized oxygen consumption and isotope tracing of uridine salvage versus de novo nucleotide synthesis. Prespecify the oxidative-high, salvage-limited subgroup independently of drug response, retaining other metabolic groups as specificity controls. Establish an exposure ceiling from verified human unbound pharmacokinetics; measure free drug in culture and mouse marrow rather than equating nominal concentrations with exposure. Use uridine concentrations reflecting measured extracellular conditions, not artificially nutrient-depleted medium.\n\nCompare vehicle, teriflunomide, an independently validated DHODH inhibitor, and partial genetic DHODH suppression. Include venetoclax–azacitidine as the established benchmark [S3], and normal CD34-positive progenitors under comparable proliferative conditions. Measure early de novo nucleotide flux and nucleotide pools, followed by absolute viable-cell counts, morphology, myeloid maturation markers, and colony output after washout. Confirm that mature-appearing cells retain the leukemia genotype, excluding enrichment of surviving normal cells. Uridine supplementation should reverse the phenotype if nucleotide shortage is causal; interpret this rescue only when tracing demonstrates restored nucleotide pools. Measure respiration concurrently to distinguish nucleotide restriction from an early, generalized respiratory collapse.\n\nFunctional validation. In randomized, blinded xenograft experiments, administer teriflunomide after cytarabine and quantify residual disease and differentiation. Transplant equal numbers of viable residual cells after washout into untreated recipients using limiting dilution, separating durable loss of leukemia propagation from transient suppression or drug carryover. Assess normal progenitor colony formation and long-term engraftment in parallel. The predicted pattern is target engagement preceding genotype-confirmed maturation and reduced propagation, preferentially in the prespecified subgroup.\n\nAlternatives, falsification, and limitations. Apparent differentiation could reflect selective death of immature cells; colony reduction could represent reversible cytostasis; oxidative status could merely track chemotherapy stress. Physiological uridine salvage might eliminate activity, and normal progenitors might share the dependency. A decisive falsifying observation would be verified DHODH inhibition and suppressed de novo nucleotide production at permitted exposure, without durable maturation or reduced leukemia propagation in the prespecified subgroup. Persistence of the phenotype despite confirmed nucleotide replenishment would instead argue against the proposed mechanism. Equal normal-cell injury would reject the selectivity prediction. Patient heterogeneity, uncertain marrow exposure, and imperfect xenograft maturation environments limit translation; activity only above tolerable exposure would not support clinical repurposing.",
